    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Social capital theory originated with Bourdieu's emphasis on cultural capital, distinguishing structural and relational forms explored by Coleman and Granovetter, while Putnam later consolidated a focus on civic engagement in bridging and bonding ties. Theoretical refinements emerged as Burt argued for structural holes filling structural voids, while Adler and Woolcock highlighted an agency-infused context where individuals leverage networks for action. Further diverse insights were provided by Portes on how networks can restrict economic advantage, alongside Marwell's work on collective problem-solving and the political economy of social capital. Institutional analysis is deepened by Scott and DiMaggio identifying how formal and informal structures shape group outcomes, while Fouzy and Field examine the normative and cognitive dimensions of social coordination. Broadening the scope, Dewatripont and Loury introduced transaction cost perspectives that influenced how social assets are evaluated in economic markets. Contributions from Cotton and Ward expanded the discussion to development contexts and bottom-up governance, respectively. A critical lens is offered by Danah-Zohar (part of the evolving discourse on negative externalities) and Van der Lippe through empirical investigations of gender dynamics within networks, alongside Warschauer and Cohen on technology-mediated capital. The field is further anchored by cosmopolitan urban studies as",
      "source": "target",
